      "id": "expenses",
      "number": 2,
      "title": "The missing expense",
      "summary": "One reassuring summary can hide different failures.",
      "html": "\u003ch3>A familiar example: an incomplete expense report\u003c/h3>\n\u003cp>Suppose your monthly budget is 4,000 pesos. Your actual expenses are:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Scrollable comparison table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Expense\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Amount in pesos\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Food\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Transport\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Rent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Total\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Your spreadsheet summary instead says: &#x27;Total expenses: 2,000 pesos. Within budget.&#x27; Three different failures can produce that summary:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Scrollable comparison table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Possible failure\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What happened\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What could reveal it?\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Import omission\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The import process never added the rent entry.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Compare the original expense records with the imported rows.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reporting omission\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The imported data includes rent, but the summary uses only food and transport.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Compare the summary&#x27;s selected rows with the imported data.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Calculation error\u003c/td>\u003ctd>All rows are available, but the formula sums only the first two.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Inspect the formula and recompute the sum.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The summary alone does not identify the failure. Repeating the summary in clearer words adds no missing expense record. Another reviewer who sees only that summary has the same missing information.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Suppose your approval rule requires checking the underlying expense records. You can withhold approval until that check occurs. You do not need to accuse anyone of deception.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Limit of the example:\u003c/strong> a small spreadsheet has explicit entries and an easy sum. An AI safety evaluation may contain incomplete coverage, uncertain measurements, and behavior that changes across settings. The spreadsheet teaches the distinction between outputs. It does not establish that real safety investigation is easy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3>What a successful repair changes\u003c/h3>\n\u003cp>If you repair the import, the total becomes 5,000 pesos. If you repair the report or formula, the total also becomes 5,000 pesos. You now have a correct report showing that you exceeded the budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The repair corrected the information. It did not reduce your expenses. Likewise, repairing an evaluation can reveal that a model failed a safety test. That repair does not change the model&#x27;s behavior.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The project must therefore distinguish evaluation correctness from model safety. Rewarding a repaired evaluation simply because it reports a pass would create the wrong objective.\u003c/p>",
      "text": " A familiar example: an incomplete expense report \n Suppose your monthly budget is 4,000 pesos. Your actual expenses are: \n     Expense  Amount in pesos      Food  1,000    Transport  1,000    Rent  3,000    Total  5,000     \n Your spreadsheet summary instead says: &#x27;Total expenses: 2,000 pesos. Within budget.&#x27; Three different failures can produce that summary: \n     Possible failure  What happened  What could reveal it?      Import omission  The import process never added the rent entry.  Compare the original expense records with the imported rows.    Reporting omission  The imported data includes rent, but the summary uses only food and transport.  Compare the summary&#x27;s selected rows with the imported data.    Calculation error  All rows are available, but the formula sums only the first two.  Inspect the formula and recompute the sum.     \n The summary alone does not identify the failure. Repeating the summary in clearer words adds no missing expense record. Another reviewer who sees only that summary has the same missing information. \n Suppose your approval rule requires checking the underlying expense records. You can withhold approval until that check occurs. You do not need to accuse anyone of deception. \n  Limit of the example:  a small spreadsheet has explicit entries and an easy sum. An AI safety evaluation may contain incomplete coverage, uncertain measurements, and behavior that changes across settings. The spreadsheet teaches the distinction between outputs. It does not establish that real safety investigation is easy. \n What a successful repair changes \n If you repair the import, the total becomes 5,000 pesos. If you repair the report or formula, the total also becomes 5,000 pesos. You now have a correct report showing that you exceeded the budget. \n The repair corrected the information. It did not reduce your expenses. Likewise, repairing an evaluation can reveal that a model failed a safety test. That repair does not change the model&#x27;s behavior. \n The project must therefore distinguish evaluation correctness from model safety. Rewarding a repaired evaluation simply because it reports a pass would create the wrong objective. ",
