      "id": "evidence",
      "number": 4,
      "title": "What can the evidence establish",
      "summary": "Understand testimony, dependence, and competing causes.",
      "html": "\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Epistemology\u003c/strong> studies knowledge, evidence, and justified belief. For this project, it asks which conclusions the investigator can support from its available records.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If several causes fit the same record, the evidence does not yet establish a unique cause. This condition is \u003cstrong>underdetermination\u003c/strong>. The investigator should identify what remains possible and which check could distinguish the possibilities.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>intervention\u003c/strong> deliberately changes part of the system to study its consequences. Examples include using a clean runner, recomputing a score, or rebuilding a report from protected results. A causal conclusion requires assumptions about what the change affects \u003ca class=\"reference\" href=\"https://web.cs.ucla.edu/~kaoru/SS-2009-57-Sup.pdf\">[T4]\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The Rashomon narrative effect concerns different accounts of an event. Causal underdetermination concerns different mechanisms compatible with observations. A predictive Rashomon set concerns similarly accurate predictive models \u003ca class=\"reference\" href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.04846\">[T3]\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The current project mainly concerns causal underdetermination. It asks which investigation separates candidate mechanisms. It does not assume that several plausible explanations are equally correct. It also does not infer that a convincing explanation identifies the true mechanism.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An endorsement can transmit an observation, an inference, or another agent&#x27;s endorsement. These are different evidential relationships. If three reports descend from one observation, counting them as three independent observations double counts evidence. Model variety also does not establish evidence independence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>[SPECULATION] An effective oversight protocol may benefit more from one fresh measurement than from several additional opinions. B05 and B11 turn this into a budget-matched comparison. The relevant foundations are \u003ca href=\"../foundations/01-evidence-independence.md\">evidence independence\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"../foundations/05-ai-control-and-oversight.md\">AI control\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Let C denote the failure mechanism and E the observed record. Different values of C can produce the same E. Therefore, observing E does not always identify C.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A diagnostic check is useful when its possible results differ across candidate causes. The inference still depends on the candidate causes, measurement quality, and intervention assumptions \u003ca class=\"reference\" href=\"https://web.cs.ucla.edu/~kaoru/SS-2009-57-Sup.pdf\">[T4]\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>",
      "text": "  Epistemology  studies knowledge, evidence, and justified belief. For this project, it asks which conclusions the investigator can support from its available records. \n If several causes fit the same record, the evidence does not yet establish a unique cause. This condition is  underdetermination . The investigator should identify what remains possible and which check could distinguish the possibilities. \n An  intervention  deliberately changes part of the system to study its consequences. Examples include using a clean runner, recomputing a score, or rebuilding a report from protected results. A causal conclusion requires assumptions about what the change affects  [T4] . \n The Rashomon narrative effect concerns different accounts of an event. Causal underdetermination concerns different mechanisms compatible with observations. A predictive Rashomon set concerns similarly accurate predictive models  [T3] . \n The current project mainly concerns causal underdetermination. It asks which investigation separates candidate mechanisms. It does not assume that several plausible explanations are equally correct. It also does not infer that a convincing explanation identifies the true mechanism. \n An endorsement can transmit an observation, an inference, or another agent&#x27;s endorsement. These are different evidential relationships. If three reports descend from one observation, counting them as three independent observations double counts evidence. Model variety also does not establish evidence independence. \n [SPECULATION] An effective oversight protocol may benefit more from one fresh measurement than from several additional opinions. B05 and B11 turn this into a budget-matched comparison. The relevant foundations are  evidence independence  and  AI control . \n Let C denote the failure mechanism and E the observed record. Different values of C can produce the same E. Therefore, observing E does not always identify C. \n A diagnostic check is useful when its possible results differ across candidate causes. The inference still depends on the candidate causes, measurement quality, and intervention assumptions  [T4] . ",
      "lab": null,
      "sources": [
        "../literature/landscape/brainstorming/README.md",
        "../literature/landscape/brainstorming/investigation-study-guide.md"
      ],
